  {
    "description": "Send the ticket you hold back to preparing, for a human to reshape before anyone works it -- when it is not ready to build: the spec is wrong, too big, or not what is needed. Posts the reason as your comment and releases the ticket. Only your own held ticket; to flag a problem with another, comment on it. For a question you need answered to carry on, use ticket_discuss instead.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "reason": {
          "description": "why the ticket needs a human to reshape it before anyone works it; posted as your comment",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "reason"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "name": "ticket_prepare",
    "outputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "human_message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "notice": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "ok": {
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "ok"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
  {
    "description": "Hand your ticket back to the queue. Posts the reason as your comment (nothing when it is blank). Waiting on another ticket: pass blocked_on, and it reopens when that ticket is done. Waiting on something the queue cannot see (a deploy not yet rolled out, an outside system's state) or a transient failure: pass retry_after, a duration like 30m, and nobody can claim the ticket until then; the reason must say what you checked, how (the command or evidence) and what would show it is met, so the next check is one step. A release naming neither blocked_on nor retry_after still keeps the ticket out of the queue for a short minimum, so an immediate re-claim is not a loop; after a few such releases in a row the server moves the ticket to discussion itself. Both together: it reopens when both clear. A release mid-work should also pass a handoff naming the branch and the pushed commit, as ticket_finish asks: whoever claims it next may be on another checkout. Omit the handoff and the previous one stays the latest. Waiting on a person's action or decision is ticket_discuss, not a release: it parks the ticket until they answer.",
    "inputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "blocked_on": {
          "description": "ticket number this is waiting on",
          "type": [
            "null",
            "integer"
          ]
        },
        "handoff": {
          "description": "where the work stands, for whoever claims it next: branch, pushed commit, what is done and half done. Omit it to leave the previous handoff as the latest",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "reason": {
          "description": "why you are handing this back; posted as your comment unless blank",
          "type": "string"
        },
        "retry_after": {
          "description": "how long to keep the ticket out of the queue before anyone may claim it again, e.g. 30m or 2h; the server clamps it (default 5m to 24h). Omitted, you get the minimum",
          "type": "string"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "reason"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    },
    "name": "ticket_release",
    "outputSchema": {
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "properties": {
        "human_message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "message": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "notice": {
          "type": "string"
        },
        "ok": {
          "type": "boolean"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "ok"
      ],
      "type": "object"
    }
  },
